description:
At the moment, we only have one node module for our cloud sdks: fh-mbaas-api.
The approach has caused a few problems that are not easy to resolve:
1. High memory usages for simple apps. Many of our template cloud apps are relatively simple and doesn't really doing anything. However, they are still using around 100MB memory, most of which are used by fh-mbaas-api.
2. Hard for app developers to maintain backward compatibility. For example, if a developer want to update the version of fh-mbaas-api for small bug fixes for certain features, it will be hard for him/her to figure out which version to update, to make sure no other features will be affected.
3. Hard for module developers to add new features in the future. If more and more features are added, this module will become bigger and bigger, and eventually it will be very hard to maintain.
The proposal here is to consider change the cloud sdks into a pluggable architecture. This will be very similar to how middlewares are included for expressjs. 
We could make the cloud sdk work like this:
* 1 core module that will handle some of the core functions. The core module should be stable and handle minium functions.
* multiple modules for feature APIs - e.g. push, sync, forms etc
For developers, they will use the cloud sdks like this (just an example):
{code:javascript}
var fh = require('fh-mbaas-api-core');
var forms = require('fh-mbaas-api-forms');
fh.use(forms);
fh.forms({})
{code}

